#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# quotient_remainder

#% text
# `quotient_remainder(m, n)` gives the list `[quotient(m, n), mod(m, n)]`.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# The quotient rounds towards `-inf` and the remainder has the sign of `n`, so `m == n*q + r` for the pair `[q, r]`.

#% item
# Integers, rationals and machine numbers are accepted.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
quotient_remainder(17, 5)
#% output
#= [3, 2]

#% cell
quotient_remainder(-17, 5)
#% output
#= [-4, 3]

#% cell
quotient_remainder(17, -5)
#% output
#= [-4, -3]

#% subsection
# Applications

#% text
# Hours and minutes from a number of minutes:

#% cell
quotient_remainder(1000, 60)
#% output
#= [16, 40]

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# The pair reconstructs the dividend:

#% cell
qr = quotient_remainder(100, 7); 7*qr[1] + qr[2]
#% output
#= 100

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `quotient`, `mod`, `integer_digits`
